menu "Packet Sniffer Configuration"

    config SNIFFER_CHANNEL_HOPPING
        bool "Enable channel hopping"
        default y
        help
            Wi-Fi: hop through the channels of the bands below (2.4 GHz 1 to SNIFFER_2G_MAX_CHANNEL;
            5 GHz 36-165), staying SNIFFER_HOP_INTERVAL_MS on each, starting from the lowest. When disabled the sniffer stays on
            SNIFFER_START_CHANNEL. (802.15.4 always starts hopping 11-26; Bluetooth LE has nothing
            to hop.)
            The host can override this at run time with the "CHANNELS <spec>" command;
            the Kismet helpers send one channel at a time as Kismet hops.

    config SNIFFER_HOP_INTERVAL_MS
        int "Channel hopping interval (ms)"
        range 50 60000
        default 250
        help
            Time spent on each channel before moving to the next one. The host can change it at
            run time with "DWELL <ms>", which allows 20 to 60000.

    config SNIFFER_START_CHANNEL
        int "Start channel"
        range 36 177 if SNIFFER_BAND_5G
        range 1 14 if SNIFFER_BAND_2G
        range 1 177
        default 36 if SNIFFER_BAND_5G
        default 1
        help
            The Wi-Fi channel the sniffer stays on after boot when hopping is disabled, until the
            host asks for another. With hopping enabled it is not used.
            It must be a channel of the band selected below, otherwise the build fails.

    config SNIFFER_2G_MAX_CHANNEL
        int "Highest 2.4 GHz channel to hop through"
        range 1 14
        default 13
        help
            The 2.4 GHz part of the hop list is 1..this value. The original Arduino sketch used 11.

    choice SNIFFER_BAND
        prompt "Bands to sniff"
        default SNIFFER_BAND_DUAL if SOC_WIFI_SUPPORT_5G
        default SNIFFER_BAND_2G
        help
            The ESP32-C5 radio is dual band but tunes to one channel at a time, so hopping over both
            bands makes a full lap over the channel list take longer.

        config SNIFFER_BAND_2G
            bool "2.4 GHz only"
        config SNIFFER_BAND_5G
            bool "5 GHz only"
            depends on SOC_WIFI_SUPPORT_5G
        config SNIFFER_BAND_DUAL
            bool "2.4 GHz + 5 GHz"
            depends on SOC_WIFI_SUPPORT_5G
    endchoice

    choice SNIFFER_LINKTYPE
        prompt "PCAP link type"
        default SNIFFER_LINKTYPE_RADIOTAP
        help
            Link type announced in the PCAP global header in Wi-Fi mode (802.15.4 is always 283,
            Bluetooth LE 256). The Kismet helpers need radiotap (127) and never start capturing
            from a board that announces 105.

        config SNIFFER_LINKTYPE_RADIOTAP
            bool "802.11 + radiotap header (127)"
            help
                Every frame is prefixed with a radiotap header carrying the channel, RSSI and noise floor,
                so Wireshark can show on which channel each frame was captured.
        config SNIFFER_LINKTYPE_IEEE802_11
            bool "Plain 802.11 (105), as in the Arduino sketch"
    endchoice

    config SNIFFER_CAPTURE_CTRL_FRAMES
        bool "Capture control frames (ACK, RTS, CTS, Block Ack, ...)"
        default y
        help
            With this enabled all three 802.11 frame types are captured: management, control and data.
            Control frames are short but very frequent, roughly one ACK per data frame. The
            USB-Serial-JTAG link carries a few hundred kB/s, so on a busy channel this raises the
            number of frames dropped for lack of bandwidth. Turn it off to capture management and
            data frames only.

    config SNIFFER_RINGBUF_SIZE
        int "Size of the capture ring buffer (bytes)"
        range 32768 262144
        default 65536
        help
            Frames wait here between the radio driver's callback and the USB writer task.
            The lower bound leaves room for the longest 802.11 frame.

    config SNIFFER_USB_TX_BUF_SIZE
        int "Size of the USB-Serial-JTAG driver TX buffer (bytes)"
        range 16384 131072
        default 32768
        help
            Each record goes to the USB port in one all-or-nothing write, so this buffer has to
            hold the longest one; the build fails otherwise.

endmenu
